Step-by-Step Solution
Step 1: Initial Binary Analysis with file command
bash

file vuln

Output:
text

vuln: ELF 64-bit LSB executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, BuildID[sha1]=19251d430d5dd4b44a3e8489a8c76f1894676f7d, for GNU/Linux 3.2.0, not stripped

This tells us:

    Architecture: 64-bit

    Linking: Dynamic

    Stripped status: Not stripped

Step 2: Source Code Analysis
bash

cat vuln.c

c

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void win(){
        system("cat flag.txt");
}

void vuln(){
        char buffer[0x15] = {0};
        fprintf(stdout, "\nEnter payload: ");
        fgets(buffer, 0x90, stdin);
}

void main(){
        vuln();
}

Key observations:

    Buffer size: 0x15 bytes

    fgets reads: 0x90 bytes

    win() function exists but is never called

    Clear buffer overflow vulnerability

Step 3: Check Security Protections
bash

checksec --file=vuln

Output:
text

RELRO           STACK CANARY      NX            PIE             RPATH      RUNPATH      Symbols         FORTIFY Fortified       Fortifiable     FILE
Partial RELRO   No canary found   NX enabled    No PIE          No RPATH   No RUNPATH   40 Symbols        No    0               1               vuln

Only NX (Non-Executable stack) is enabled.
Step 4: Find win() Function Address
bash

objdump -d vuln | grep win

Output:
text

0000000000401176 <win>:

win() address: 0x401176
